# Phase 30 Plan 01: inspect_network_policy MCP capability

## Goal

### Problem

Release builds can fail plain HTTP requests even when device connectivity and backend reachability are healthy, because iOS ATS and Android cleartext policy block unapproved HTTP domains. The current network tools probe runtime connectivity, but they do not inspect release policy configuration.

### Expected Outcome

- [x] A new `inspect_network_policy` MCP tool returns structured Android/iOS policy findings for provided HTTP URLs or domains.
- [x] Android checks cover `usesCleartextTraffic`, `network_security_config`, `cleartextTrafficPermitted`, and domain-specific allow rules when readable XML config is provided.
- [x] iOS checks cover `NSAppTransportSecurity`, `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`, `NSExceptionDomains`, `NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads`, and `NSTemporaryExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads` when readable plist data is provided.
- [x] The result distinguishes `allowed`, `blocked`, `unknown`, and `not_applicable` so AI agents do not confuse missing evidence with policy failure.
- [x] Capability reporting and README tool lists include the new release policy inspection boundary.

### Non-goals

- Runtime traffic interception, proxy setup, latency/error injection, or response mocking.
- Backend health checks; `probe_network_readiness` remains the runtime connectivity tool.
- Automatic mutation of `AndroidManifest.xml`, `network_security_config.xml`, or `Info.plist`.
- Full binary Android manifest decoding without local Android tooling. If an APK cannot be decoded deterministically, return `unknown` with remediation guidance.

## Plan

### Strategy

Add an additive, read-only capability with deterministic parsing first. The implementation should work reliably with source/config files, read XML policy evidence directly from ZIP-based APK/IPA artifacts when available, and gracefully report conditional support when binary artifact decoding is unavailable. Keep policy analysis pure and unit-testable in `adapter-maestro`; wire MCP/tool contracts separately.

### Read First

- `AGENTS.md`
- `docs/engineering/ai-first-capability-expansion-guideline.md`
- `.planning/PLANNING-PROTOCOL.md`
- `packages/adapter-maestro/src/network-probe.ts`
- `packages/adapter-maestro/src/capability-model.ts`
- `packages/mcp-server/src/index.ts`
- `packages/mcp-server/src/server.ts`
- `packages/contracts/src/types.ts`
- `packages/contracts/src/constants/tool-names.ts`

### Task Breakdown

1. Add contract types for `InspectNetworkPolicyInput`, `InspectNetworkPolicyData`, platform policy findings, endpoint findings, and evidence records.
2. Add `TOOL_NAMES.inspectNetworkPolicy` and export the new types from contracts.
3. Write failing adapter tests for Android cleartext defaults, Android domain allowlist, iOS ATS defaults, iOS exception domains, HTTPS not-applicable handling, and missing-evidence unknown status.
4. Implement `packages/adapter-maestro/src/network-policy-inspection.ts` with pure parsing/evaluation helpers and a tool-level wrapper.
5. Add MCP server handler, tool contract map entry, registry descriptor, stdio tool list entry, and capability model entries.
6. Add tool-data schema coverage if this tool is promoted into the schema validation set.
7. Update README / README.zh-CN network diagnostics tool lists and support caveats.
8. Write summary and verification artifacts, then sync `STATE.md` and `ROADMAP.md`.

### Risks / Unknowns

- Android APK manifests are binary XML in normal builds; deterministic decoding may require `aapt`, `aapt2`, or `apkanalyzer`. MVP must not pretend unsupported extraction is a policy result.
- Info.plist may be binary; macOS `plutil` can decode it, but direct plist path tests should stay pure and deterministic.
- `NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent` does not allow native networking; the tool should not over-report it as general HTTP allow.
- Matching Android subdomains and iOS exception-domain semantics must be explicit and conservative.

### Done Criteria

- [x] New MCP tool is listed by `server.listTools()` and stdio `list_tools`.
- [x] Tool invocation returns structured findings and next suggestions.
- [x] Unit tests prove Android and iOS allowed/blocked/unknown cases.
- [x] Capability profile marks the tool as read-only and available without session.
- [x] README and README.zh-CN expose the new release policy inspection capability.
- [x] Planning summary and verification artifacts capture evidence.

## Implement

### Planned Changes

- `packages/contracts/src/types.ts` — add input/output data contracts.
- `packages/contracts/src/constants/tool-names.ts` — add canonical tool name.
- `packages/contracts/src/index.ts` — export new contracts.
- `packages/adapter-maestro/src/network-policy-inspection.ts` — implement policy parsing and evaluation.
- `packages/adapter-maestro/src/index.ts` — export implementation.
- `packages/adapter-maestro/src/capability-model.ts` — expose capability in Android/iOS profiles.
- `packages/adapter-maestro/test/network-policy-inspection.test.ts` — add TDD tests.
- `packages/mcp-server/src/tools/inspect-network-policy.ts` — bridge to adapter implementation.
- `packages/mcp-server/src/server.ts` — add contract map entry.
- `packages/mcp-server/src/index.ts` — register tool descriptor.
- `packages/mcp-server/src/stdio-server.ts` — list stdio tool.
- `packages/mcp-server/test/server.test.ts` and `stdio-server.test.ts` — assert listing/invocation surface.
- `README.md` and `README.zh-CN.md` — document the capability.

### Key Decisions To Preserve

- This is static release-policy inspection, not active network probing.
- Missing or undecodable config produces `unknown`, not `allowed`.
- HTTPS endpoints are `not_applicable` because ATS/cleartext allowlisting only concerns plain HTTP.
- iOS web-content-only ATS exceptions must not be treated as native networking permission.
- Android domain-specific network security config overrides are only reported when the referenced config XML is available.

## Verify

### Test Cases

- [x] Android default release policy blocks HTTP when cleartext is not enabled.
- [x] Android `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` allows HTTP globally.
- [x] Android network security config allows only matching domains.
